//! fleet_dev — one brief, planned into legs by a planning counsel, built by
//! parallel delegated builders in isolated worktrees, merged mechanically,
//! gated in code, fix-rounds routed back to the dev seat deterministically,
//! judged by a judging counsel, and pushed home with a receipt — on the
//! engine's own durability, resident on a fleet box, with every seam proven
//! against a known answer before anything real is spent.
//!
//! This is the box edition. The original was authored for home use, where the
//! server and the working tree share one machine and `run` bodies execute on
//! the server against a tree it can reach. On a fleet box the tree lives with
//! the workers, so every mechanical action here is BODILESS and served by the
//! `fleet_ops` worker document (`aion worker awl`) on the box — and because a
//! worker `run json` action treats a nonzero exit as an activity failure and
//! DISCARDS its stdout, every sealed script exits zero on its anticipated
//! faces and carries the INNER command's exit code back as typed data. The
//! workflow routes on that typed field; a red gate is a routed finding, never
//! a retried-then-terminal failure with its evidence thrown away.
//!
//! THE TREE IS THE BLACKBOARD, AND THE BLACKBOARD IS COMMITTED. AWL composes
//! no strings at runtime — so every prompt in this document is an ADDRESS
//! into the working tree, and every deliverable travels IN COMMITS (design
//! law, Tom's row): the planner commits `.fleet/plan.md` and one brief per
//! leg under `.fleet/legs/`; builders commit their work and their report
//! under `.fleet/reports/`; the gate script commits its red findings whole
//! to `.fleet/gates/` with a stable `RED-LATEST.md` address for the fix
//! round. A committed plan has a hash: it can be cited, diffed, and audited.
//! The law also pays mechanically — `git worktree add` carries only
//! committed state, so fan-out needs no copying, and fan-in is ordered
//! merges, because the reports ride the leg branches home.
//!
//! CHECKS RUN IN CODE; EXACT FEEDBACK ROUTES BACK; DECISIONS THAT CAN BE
//! RULES ARE RULES (Tom's three clauses, binding). `plan_check` and
//! `report_check` verify deliverables against HEAD, not eloquence.
//! The gate step is a BOUNDED LOOP: a red gate's findings — the failing
//! tests, the clippy output, verbatim — are committed to the tree by the
//! gate script and the fix round routes the dev seat at that address with
//! no human relay; three gate visits exhaust to `gates_red` with the
//! evidence in the payload. The visit bound is structural (a literal, by
//! AWL's grammar); making it a dispatch input is named on the row as a
//! candidate engine extension. The dispatcher's `repo` address is checked
//! against the provisioner's own derivation — an addressing disagreement
//! refuses before any agent is spent.
//!
//! FAILURE KEEPS THE TREE; SUCCESS PUSHES THEN CLEANS. Every failure route
//! carries `kept_root`: the run tree is the evidence and the salvage, and
//! deletion inherits every adjective of its justification — a failed fleet's
//! branches are work, and work is never destroyed in passing. The success
//! route pushes the merged branch home with a typed receipt first, and only
//! then tears down; a teardown refusal rides the report as data rather than
//! failing a run whose work is already home.
//!
//! WHAT THIS DOES NOT PROVE: a green gate proves what the gate entry
//! measures; a judge's CLEAR is one model's read of the tree, priced by its
//! charter but unverified. The landing re-gate and the landing itself stay
//! at the operator's desk, after the pushed branch arrives.
workflow fleet_dev
  input workspace_root: String
  input run_name: String
  input repo_url: String
  input base_ref: String
  input result_branch: String
  input repo: String
  input leg_ids_csv: String
  input gate_entry: String
  input counsel_control_prompt: String
  input counsel_control_expect: String
  input control_prompt: String
  input control_expect: String
  input plan_prompt: String
  input fix_prompt: String
  input legs: [Leg]
  input judge_prompt: String
  input judge_pass: String
  outcome done:          type FleetDevReport, route success
  outcome unprovisioned: type Unprovisioned,  route failure
  outcome unmeasured:    type Refused,        route failure
  outcome unplanned:     type PlanRefused,    route failure
  outcome unforked:      type Unforked,       route failure
  outcome unmerged:      type Unmerged,       route failure
  outcome unreported:    type Unreported,     route failure
  outcome gates_red:     type GatesRed,       route failure
  outcome judge_flagged: type JudgeFlagged,   route failure
  outcome unpushed:      type Unpushed,       route failure

/// One unit of fleet work. `prompt` is complete and self-contained AS AN
/// ADDRESS: it names the leg's brief file the planner committed and the
/// report file the builder must commit, plus the standing register (floors
/// not ceilings, hard walls restated). `repo` is the leg's OWN isolated
/// worktree at the deterministic address `<root>/legs/<id>` — the
/// dispatcher composes it, and `fanout` cuts it there or refuses.
type Leg { id: String, prompt: String, repo: String }

/// The canonical agent-outcome record every dispatch of an agent seam
/// returns: the whole accumulated `text`, the last answer alone as
/// `final_message`, the canonical `stop_reason`, and the `session_id`
/// handle of the conversation that produced it.
type Reply { text: String, final_message: String, stop_reason: String, session_id: String }

/// A sealed mechanical command's result, carried as DATA: `exit_code` is the
/// INNER command's status (the script itself exits zero on every anticipated
/// face), and the streams are bounded tails with the full log at a named
/// path in the run tree. The workflow routes on `exit_code`.
type OpProbe { exit_code: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String }

/// The provisioner's receipt: the run root and repo path it derived (checked
/// against the dispatcher's own addressing), and the HEAD it checked out.
type CloneProbe { ok: Bool, refusal: String, root: String, repo_path: String, head_commit: String }

/// The delivery receipt: which commit landed on which branch of which
/// remote. A refusal names its face — non-fast-forward, auth, unreachable —
/// and the run tree is kept.
type PushProbe { ok: Bool, refusal: String, branch: String, pushed_commit: String, remote: String }

/// The cleanup receipt. Refusal is data on the success report, never a run
/// failure: the work is already home when this runs.
type TeardownProbe { ok: Bool, removed: String, refusal: String }

/// A fleet that planned, built in parallel, merged clean, gated green
/// (possibly through fix rounds), was judged clear, and pushed home. The
/// builders' own accounts travel with the machine's receipts and the
/// judge's admission — an assertion that the method worked is not a
/// demonstration.
type FleetDevReport {
  plan: String,
  leg_ids: [String],
  reports: [String],
  verdict: String,
  base_commit: String,
  pushed_commit: String,
  result_branch: String,
  gate_exit: Int,
  control_reply: String,
  counsel_control_reply: String,
  teardown_ok: Bool,
  teardown_refusal: String,
}

/// The tree could not be provisioned — or the dispatcher's `repo` address
/// and the provisioner's derivation disagree, which refuses before any
/// agent is spent.
type Unprovisioned { refusal: String, kept_root: String }

/// A fleet that did not build, said out loud rather than reported as a
/// pass: a control seat answered something other than the known answer.
type Refused { control_expect: String, control_reply: String, reason: String, kept_root: String }

/// The planner answered but its promised deliverable is not committed at
/// HEAD. Carries the planner's account beside the check's own streams.
type PlanRefused { plan: String, check_exit: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String, kept_root: String }

/// The leg worktrees could not be cut, so no builder was spent. The fanout
/// script refuses loudly — including when a leftover tree or branch from a
/// prior attempt is in the way, which is the dispatcher's to inspect,
/// never a script's to delete.
type Unforked { plan: String, fork_exit: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String, kept_root: String }

/// The legs built but their branches could not be merged back. A conflict
/// here is a finding about the plan's leg boundaries — two briefs claimed
/// the same ground. The script names the conflicting files on stdout and
/// aborts the merge, leaving the primary tree clean and every leg branch
/// intact in the kept tree.
type Unmerged { plan: String, reports: [String], merge_exit: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String, kept_root: String }

/// The builders answered but at least one promised report is not committed
/// in the merged tree. Caught by a script before the gates, never left for
/// the judge as a mechanism of last resort.
type Unreported { plan: String, reports: [String], check_exit: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String, kept_root: String }

/// The legs built and merged but the gates stayed red through every fix
/// round. The final round's findings travel here, and the full history
/// of every round is committed in the kept tree under `.fleet/gates/`.
type GatesRed { plan: String, reports: [String], gate_exit: Int, stdout: String, stderr: String, kept_root: String }

/// The delivery gated green and the judge refused it anyway — or answered
/// anything other than the exact admission token. The whole verdict text
/// travels with the refusal; the mismatch direction is deliberate: this
/// document can produce a false flag, never a false clear.
type JudgeFlagged { plan: String, reports: [String], verdict: String, kept_root: String }

/// Judged clear but the push home was refused. The verdict and the named
/// refusal travel together; the tree is kept — the work exists only there.
type Unpushed { verdict: String, push_refusal: String, kept_root: String }

worker counsel
  /// The planning and judging seats, one Claude-backed ACP worker on queue
  /// `counsel`, RESIDENT on the fleet box under its standing auth (Tom's
  /// word, verified at the bytes: ~/.claude + ~/.claude.json present).
  /// Prompts go to the session verbatim; replies come back verbatim; the
  /// working tree arrives with each dispatch.
  ///
  /// `command` is the box's bun runner — the box has no node/npx, and bunx
  /// resolves and runs the adapter (0.69.0 preflight, rc=0). The seated
  /// handshake is proven by `counsel_control` before anything is spent.
  /// `permission "allow-once"` because the planner WRITES AND COMMITS its
  /// deliverables into the tree; the reconnect ladder is the proven norn
  /// register's.
  harness
    kind acp
    concurrency 1
    reconnect_initial_backoff 1s
    reconnect_max_backoff 30s
    reconnect_max_attempts 100
    env_pass "PATH", "HOME", "USER", "LOGNAME", "TMPDIR"
    command "/home/aion/.bun/bin/bunx"
    args "@agentclientprotocol/claude-agent-acp"
    cwd from repo
    permission "allow-once"
    exit_grace 30s
  /// The planner. Reads the dispatcher's brief (carried whole in the
  /// prompt), grounds every requirement file:line in the tree, writes
  /// `.fleet/plan.md` plus one complete builder brief per leg, and COMMITS
  /// them — an uncommitted plan fails `plan_check` by law.
  action plan(repo: String, prompt: String) -> Reply
    agent, retry 3 backoff 30s..5m
  /// The judge. Two duties, in its charter, carried in the prompt: the
  /// permitted-choice points first, the hard-wall audit second. Never the
  /// gates. Replies with exactly the admission token, or with its findings.
  action judge(repo: String, prompt: String) -> Reply
    agent, retry 3 backoff 30s..5m

worker dev
  /// The builders' seat: queue `dev`, the same action seam as `norn_dev`'s
  /// declaration so a seated norn worker serves both documents.
  /// `concurrency 3` is the parallel-legs value — one lane per leg — and
  /// the fix round rides the same seat at concurrency 1 by construction
  /// (one primary tree, one fix prompt). Same register as the pilot: the
  /// brief address goes to the session verbatim, the report comes back
  /// verbatim, retries absorb provider-overload bursts — a failed build is
  /// a reply, not an error.
  harness
    kind norn
    concurrency 3
    reconnect_initial_backoff 1s
    reconnect_max_backoff 30s
    reconnect_max_attempts 100
    env_pass "PATH", "HOME", "USER", "LOGNAME", "TMPDIR"
    args "--account", "default"
    cwd from repo
  action delegate(repo: String, prompt: String) -> Reply
    agent, retry 3 backoff 30s..5m

worker fleet_ops
  /// The mechanical half, served by `aion worker awl` on the box from
  /// `fleet_ops_worker.awl` — bodiless here, because the commands must run
  /// where the tree lives. Every action's script is sealed at
  /// `/home/aion/fleet/bin/` with its sha256 in the runbook, exits zero on
  /// anticipated faces, and carries the inner exit code as typed data.
  action clone(repo_url: String, base_ref: String, workspace_root: String, run_name: String) -> CloneProbe
  action plan_check(workspace_root: String, run_name: String, leg_ids_csv: String) -> OpProbe
  action fanout(workspace_root: String, run_name: String, leg_ids_csv: String) -> OpProbe
  action fanin(workspace_root: String, run_name: String, leg_ids_csv: String) -> OpProbe
  action report_check(workspace_root: String, run_name: String, leg_ids_csv: String) -> OpProbe
  action gates(workspace_root: String, run_name: String, gate_entry: String) -> OpProbe
  action push(workspace_root: String, run_name: String, result_branch: String) -> PushProbe
  action teardown(workspace_root: String, run_name: String) -> TeardownProbe

/// Provision the run tree: clone at the named ref under the confined run
/// root. The dispatcher's `repo` address must equal the provisioner's own
/// derivation — a rule, not a judgment, so it routes in code.
step provision
  clone(repo_url: repo_url, base_ref: base_ref, workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name) -> cloned
  outcome ready: when cloned.ok and cloned.repo_path == repo, route counsel_control
  outcome misaddressed: when cloned.ok,
    route unprovisioned(refusal: "the dispatcher's repo input does not equal the provisioned repo path — the addressing must agree before any agent is spent",
      kept_root: cloned.root)
  outcome refused: otherwise,
    route unprovisioned(refusal: cloned.refusal, kept_root: cloned.root)

/// Prove the counsel seam before spending it: ask the planning seat a
/// question whose answer the dispatcher already knows. A broken pipeline
/// and a working one both return well-formed strings; only a known answer
/// tells them apart. This is also the seated-handshake proof for the
/// resident ACP worker.
step counsel_control
  plan(repo: repo, prompt: counsel_control_prompt) -> counsel_control
  outcome proven: when counsel_control.text == counsel_control_expect, route dev_control
  outcome broken: otherwise,
    route unmeasured(control_expect: counsel_control_expect, control_reply: counsel_control.text,
      reason: "the counsel control did not return the expected reply, so the planning and judging pipeline cannot be shown to read the tree or follow instructions — nothing was planned",
      kept_root: cloned.root)

/// Prove the builder seam the same way, before the planner writes a single
/// brief for it.
step dev_control
  delegate(repo: repo, prompt: control_prompt) -> control
  outcome proven: when control.text == control_expect, route plan_legs
  outcome broken: otherwise,
    route unmeasured(control_expect: control_expect, control_reply: control.text,
      reason: "the builder control did not return the expected reply, so the delegation pipeline cannot be shown to read the tree or follow instructions — nothing was planned or built",
      kept_root: cloned.root)

step plan_legs
  plan(repo: repo, prompt: plan_prompt) -> planned

/// The planner's answer is a claim; HEAD is the fact. The check verifies
/// the plan and every leg brief exist, are non-empty, and are COMMITTED —
/// a loose working-tree plan is a red, by the blackboard-is-committed law.
step check_plan
  plan_check(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name, leg_ids_csv: leg_ids_csv) -> plan_checked
  outcome written: when plan_checked.exit_code == 0, route cut_trees
  outcome missing: otherwise,
    route unplanned(plan: planned.text, check_exit: plan_checked.exit_code,
      stdout: plan_checked.stdout, stderr: plan_checked.stderr, kept_root: cloned.root)

/// Cut the leg worktrees from the planned tree before any builder is
/// spent: a fleet that cannot be isolated does not build. Committed state
/// rides into every worktree for free — that is the law paying.
step cut_trees
  fanout(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name, leg_ids_csv: leg_ids_csv) -> forked
  outcome cut: when forked.exit_code == 0, route open
  outcome uncut: otherwise,
    route unforked(plan: planned.text, fork_exit: forked.exit_code, stdout: forked.stdout,
      stderr: forked.stderr, kept_root: cloned.root)

step open
  distribute leg in legs

step build_one
  delegate(repo: leg.repo, prompt: leg.prompt) -> built
  built.text -> report

step close
  collect report -> reports
  legs |> map(.id) -> leg_ids

/// The machine joins what the builders made: leg branches merged in
/// dispatch order, reports riding their commits home. A conflict is a
/// finding about the plan's leg boundaries and routes with the conflicting
/// files named — it is never an agent's to resolve in passing.
step merge
  fanin(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name, leg_ids_csv: leg_ids_csv) -> merged
  outcome joined: when merged.exit_code == 0, route check_reports
  outcome conflicted: otherwise,
    route unmerged(plan: planned.text, reports: reports, merge_exit: merged.exit_code,
      stdout: merged.stdout, stderr: merged.stderr, kept_root: cloned.root)

/// A builder's answer is a claim; the merged HEAD is the fact — the same
/// law `check_plan` enforces on the planner, enforced on every builder, so
/// the judge is never the first to notice an eloquent reply that wrote
/// nothing.
step check_reports
  report_check(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name, leg_ids_csv: leg_ids_csv) -> reports_checked
  outcome written: when reports_checked.exit_code == 0, route gate
  outcome missing: otherwise,
    route unreported(plan: planned.text, reports: reports, check_exit: reports_checked.exit_code,
      stdout: reports_checked.stdout, stderr: reports_checked.stderr, kept_root: cloned.root)

/// Mechanical measurement before judgement — and Tom's clause 2 in motion:
/// on red, the gate script has already COMMITTED the findings whole to
/// `.fleet/gates/` (stable address `RED-LATEST.md`, numbered history), so
/// the fix round routes the dev seat at that address with the verbatim
/// evidence and no human relay. Three visits exhaust to `gates_red`.
step gate
  gates(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name, gate_entry: gate_entry) -> gate_result
  outcome green: when gate_result.exit_code == 0, route judgement
  outcome fixable: when visits < 3, route fix_round
  outcome red: otherwise,
    route gates_red(plan: planned.text, reports: reports, gate_exit: gate_result.exit_code,
      stdout: gate_result.stdout, stderr: gate_result.stderr, kept_root: cloned.root)
  max 3 visits

/// The fix round: the dev seat, in the PRIMARY tree, at the committed
/// findings' address. Its reply is recorded like any other; the gate
/// re-measures regardless of what it claims — a fix that wrote nothing
/// meets the same red and exhausts the loop mechanically.
step fix_round
  delegate(repo: repo, prompt: fix_prompt) -> fixed
  route gate

/// The judge rules on the delivery that gated green. Exact admission only:
/// anything other than the dispatcher's `judge_pass` token — a hedge, a
/// preamble, a finding — routes to `judge_flagged` with the verdict whole.
step judgement
  judge(repo: repo, prompt: judge_prompt) -> judged
  outcome clear: when judged.text == judge_pass, route push_result
  outcome flagged: otherwise,
    route judge_flagged(plan: planned.text, reports: reports, verdict: judged.text,
      kept_root: cloned.root)

/// Deliver home with a receipt. A refusal keeps the tree — the work exists
/// only there until the remote holds it.
step push_result
  push(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name, result_branch: result_branch) -> pushed
  outcome delivered: when pushed.ok, route clean_up
  outcome refused: otherwise,
    route unpushed(verdict: judged.text, push_refusal: pushed.refusal, kept_root: cloned.root)

/// The work is home; now the tree may go. A teardown refusal is data on
/// the report, never a failure of a run whose branch is already pushed.
step clean_up
  teardown(workspace_root: workspace_root, run_name: run_name) -> td
  route done(plan: planned.text, leg_ids: leg_ids, reports: reports, verdict: judged.text,
    base_commit: cloned.head_commit, pushed_commit: pushed.pushed_commit,
    result_branch: pushed.branch, gate_exit: gate_result.exit_code,
    control_reply: control.text, counsel_control_reply: counsel_control.text,
    teardown_ok: td.ok, teardown_refusal: td.refusal)
